[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059716,
      "id": "9c45a70b-273c-5fec-a768-40a1cf218628",
      "title": "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "312e7102-b2f9-500d-903a-7d29bc199f8a",
      ":question/problem": "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$ and points the same direction.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} &= \\frac{\\mathbf{a}}{|\\mathbf{a}|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\mathbf{a}| &= \\sqrt{(12)^2+(-6)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{180} \\\\\n&= 6 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a radical denominator, use $\\sqrt{R}\\,\\sqrt{R}=R$ to simplify the components; equivalently, $1/\\sqrt{R}=\\sqrt{R}/R$ for $R>0$.\n\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} &= \\frac{\\mathbf{a}}{|\\mathbf{a}|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6 \\sqrt{5}}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6 \\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/requires-calculator": false,
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8f06cf59-62ff-5ebd-a804-09dcd47200ad",
