    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c1bb5e51-be9e-5f68-b7c4-c9e3c939170d",
      ":question/problem": "Let $\\mathbf{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}$ and points the same direction.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} &= \\frac{\\mathbf{n}}{|\\mathbf{n}|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\mathbf{n}| &= \\sqrt{(-9)^2+(-12)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225} \\\\\n&= 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{n}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} &= \\frac{\\mathbf{n}}{|\\mathbf{n}|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{15}\\cdot (\\mathbf{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{15}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
